  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "Laplace Transform of $e^{-at}u(t)$",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute the one-sided Laplace transform of $e^{-at}u(t)$ from the $0^-$-to-$\\infty$ definition, producing $X(s)=1/(s+a)$ with convergence condition $\\operatorname{Re}(s)>-a$ as the base exponential transform pair.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Laplace Transform of $e^{-at}u(t)$",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Laplace Transform of $e^{-at}u(t)$",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The target move is to identify the one-sided transform setup for $x(t)=e^{-at}u(t)$ and turn it into a reusable exponential transform pair.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the base cue as the causal exponential step: in this topic, $u(t)$ is the on-off switch that is $1$ for $t\\ge0$ and $0$ for $t<0$, so the one-sided transform uses the interval from $0^-$ to infinity.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_{+}\\{e^{-at}u(t)\\}=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty} e^{-at}u(t)e^{-st}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Drop $u(t)$ inside the interval $t\\ge0$ by substitution and combine exponents into one weighted exponential: $e^{-(s+a)t}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_{+}\\{e^{-at}u(t)\\}=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty} e^{-(s+a)t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This section stops here with setup; next sections evaluate the integral to get $1/(s+a)$ and then state the convergence condition $\\operatorname{Re}(s)>-a$ before extending to named examples.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Laplace Transform of $e^{-at}u(t)$",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Setting Up the One-Sided Integral for $e^{-at}u(t)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Set up the one-sided Laplace transform integral for $x(t)=e^{-3t}u(t)$, including all limits and factors.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the one-sided definition as the cue for this step: for any signal $x(t)$, the transform setup is $\\mathcal{L}_{+}\\{x(t)\\}=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty}x(t)e^{-st}\\,dt$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_{+}\\{x(t)\\}=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty}x(t)e^{-st}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply that rule to the specific cue $x(t)=e^{-3t}u(t)$ by substituting the signal into the template.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_{+}\\{e^{-3t}u(t)\\}=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty}e^{-3t}u(t)e^{-st}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because this is one-sided, keep the lower limit at $0^-$ and keep the $u(t)$ factor visible, so the causal-support rule is explicit before any integral evaluation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_{+}\\{e^{-3t}u(t)\\}=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty}e^{-(s+3)t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the complete setup for the next section: the concrete case $a=3$ is now written with correct limits and one-sided notation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Laplace Transform of $e^{-at}u(t)$",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Evaluating the Integral to Obtain $1/(s+a)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Evaluate $\\mathcal{L}_+\\{e^{-2t}u(t)\\}$ from the one-sided integral and simplify the result.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the one-sided setup from the previous section as the cue: evaluate the setup for the concrete signal $x(t)=e^{-2t}u(t)$ as a definite integral, not another setup.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{e^{-2t}u(t)\\}=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty} e^{-(s+2)t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Recognize the integrand as an exponential and use the antiderivative rule for $\\int e^{-(s+2)t}dt$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int e^{-(s+2)t}dt=-\\frac{1}{s+2}e^{-(s+2)t}+C",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply bounds from $0^-$ to $\\infty$ and require $\\operatorname{Re}(s)>-2$ so the upper limit term decays.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{e^{-2t}u(t)\\}=\\left[-\\frac{1}{s+2}e^{-(s+2)t}\\right]_{0^-}^{\\infty}=-\\frac{1}{s+2}(0)-\\left(-\\frac{1}{s+2}\\cdot 1\\right)=\\frac{1}{s+2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the worked result is $\\mathcal{L}_+\\{e^{-2t}u(t)\\}=1/(s+2)$, which confirms the target base-pair form for the same canonical family.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Laplace Transform of $e^{-at}u(t)$",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Determining the Convergence Condition $\\operatorname{Re}(s)>-a$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Determine the ROC for $\\mathcal{L}_+\\{e^{-at}u(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s+a}$ by using the limit behavior at infinity.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the antiderivative boundary form from the previous step as the cue, now read as a condition check rather than another algebraic manipulation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{e^{-at}u(t)\\}=\\left[-\\frac{1}{s+a}e^{-(s+a)t}\\right]_{0^-}^{\\infty}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A finite one-sided transform requires the upper-limit term to vanish, so evaluate $\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}e^{-(s+a)t}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}e^{-(s+a)t}=0 \\iff \\operatorname{Re}(s+a)>0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Convert that inequality to the ROC statement in terms of $s$ by subtracting $\\operatorname{Re}(a)=a$ (assuming real $a$ in this topic convention).",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\operatorname{Re}(s)>-a",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore the final transform statement is $\\mathcal{L}_+\\{e^{-at}u(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s+a},\\;\\operatorname{Re}(s)>-a$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Laplace Transform of $e^{-at}u(t)$",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Reading Specific Exponential Signals as $1/(s+a)$ Pairs",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Write the transform pair for $e^{-5t}u(t)$ and list its ROC.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the base-pair template as the cue: for a signal of the form $e^{-at}u(t)$, the transform is $1/(s+a)$ with the associated one-sided ROC.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_+\\{e^{-at}u(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s+a},\\qquad \\operatorname{Re}(s)>-a",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Match $e^{-5t}u(t)$ directly to the template and identify $a=5$.",
